#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:5
#: 48dbba83da7349cc9759c8feb9d3c7d2
msgid "Swarming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:8
#: d56998723a1242689f63a7184046b1ac
msgid "Swarming/Formation-Flying Interface (Beta)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:13
#: cb21c96fea8e4e84a085e765db482038
msgid "Mission Planner supports limited \"swarming\", or formation-flying with multiple UAVs.  At the moment this is in an experimental beta implementation, which is admittedly neither easy to use nor 100% reliable. However, if you're interested in multiple-UAV coordination, this is a good place to start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:19
#: 0639dd197b814165aabddd4debad0143
msgid "More sophisticated swarming/multiple-UAV control is available in QGroundControl and APM Planner 2.0, which are both built on a multiple-vehicle architecture. But Mission Planner accomplishes this in a limited way by simply opening multiple serial port MAVLink connections simultaneously (see four-UAV USB hub above) and passing GPS position information from one (\"leader\", flying in any mode, from manual to auto) to the other (\"followers\", flying in Guided mode). The GPS position of the leader is adjusted by a set offset and then sent to the followers as a series of dynamic waypoints.  In this fashion the followers will follow the leader at set X,Y and Z offset distances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:30
#: d4d497dd68d44fd7a34e7705d7d6fa69
msgid "This interface can be entered by pressing Control-F in the Mission Planner. That will bring up a pretty crowded box of buttons (see below), but the key ones are marked below: identifying the serial port of the \"follower\" UAVs, and the \"swarm\" button, which will bring up the second window, with a grid showing the relative position offset of the other UAVs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:40
#: fda55a6e69eb4ea6b64218e6768bfdcb
msgid "Mission Planner: Screen with SwarmButton"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:42
#: 37244f74229544d5a56799ea7a2588b9
msgid "**Setup Procedure:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:44
#: e1f8da79f200451d8e58ed51736fd2a2
msgid "Plug in one 3DR radio per vehicle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:45
#: a1c90370421f40b2928fcd330c491cd5
msgid "Connect to the \"leader\" in the Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:46
#: 2c0ca7b42e664c158dd0beaca155d66a
msgid "Press Control-F and click \"swarm\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:47
#: e72e39b518bd4bd89f9b431f967bcc4e
msgid "Click \"set leader\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:48
#: 93a8637b622b45ecaec353a9fc0a865c
msgid "Click \"Connect MAVs\" (\"MAV\" stands for \"micro air vehicle\"). Click it once for each MAV. As each connects via MAVLink they will appear on the grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:51
#: 98765fdb664048899941867630792288
msgid "Drag the MAV circles around the grid to set the desired offsets. The Mission Planner assumes that \"up\" is North."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:53
#: ed64fbb6f4aa493295d92d093ffff459
msgid "\"Start\" will start sending Guided Mode waypoints to all vehicles except the leader."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:56
#: 4e9677abcc1a4cc78f4b4dcacdaa1630
msgid "Now, as you may have guessed, getting the starting position can be the hardest. In the grid they are identified by the MAV number and COM port. It's a good idea to tape that MAV number to the physical vehicles themselves, so you can correspond the on-screen numbers and grid position with the physical layout of the vehicles on the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:62
#: a36d087754904404a886afee8f3c0a3f
msgid "Currently the code does no path prediction etc. A good place to start is with two quadcopters about 20 m apart to test, and work up from there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:65
#: 7d56838d21d84d55952d627ddc79e971
msgid "**First Flight:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:67
#: c9731f5e19b248a69d343a8ce00f2843
msgid "Take off with the leader, sit at say 2-10 m high (loiter). The leader flight mode doesn’t matter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:69
#: c4e1c45227df488e95838b93bba661a5
msgid "Click start. This should make the follower take off in guided mode (it will take 3-5 seconds to launch) and sit at the same altitude as the master (assuming you haven't changed the altitude offsets)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:72
#: b5f1d5b2c86845278ffc297bda825482
msgid "Manually move the leaders, and the 2nd quad (follower) should follow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:73
#: 6881bfd5a9d74000bf70c0d0818b2840
msgid "When you're done, manually land the leader. Use the \"Land\" button to tell the follower to land, too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:77
#: 75c31c7ce84d47eaa7fedaa5c9293268
msgid "**Tips**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:80
#: e5e5e12e1095495ab8c26a6020d945b7
msgid "Don't close the swarming screen, as it will stop the swarming. (Since we use guided mode, if you do the quad will just sit at the last commanded location.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:83
#: 2959572c32254e7ead016f882ea63476
msgid "The main map interface will show both quads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:84
#: ef0ba9aadfbe431dab081cdb613c5a11
msgid "If you drag the quad around the grid screen, while it's active. the quad should actually move as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/swarming.rst:86
#: 5157d6b1d57d486cb48e60cd4c536af3
msgid "Increase the position stream rate in the mp. config>planner>position. to say 5, from the default of 3. to reduce the latency"
msgstr ""
